  "seven_q": {
    "Q0_posture": "This document takes a modeling stance, proposing a mathematical isomorphism between dynamical systems theory and eschatological outcomes.",
    "Q1_identity": "An isomorphism record (ISO-016) establishing a structural mapping between attractor dynamics in physics and heaven/hell in theology.",
    "Q2_domain": "Lives at the intersection of mathematics (dynamical systems), physics (phase space analysis), and Christian theology (eschatology).",
    "Q3_claim": "Moral destiny follows a logistic-type dynamical system with two stable attractors (heaven at Phi=1, hell at Phi=0) and one unstable saddle (narrow gate at Phi=0.5), producing a bimodal distribution of final states with no neutral terminus.",
    "Q4_support": "Grounded in the logistic equation (Verhulst), phase portrait analysis, biblical witness (Matthew 25, Luke 16), and connections to ISO-013 (Grace Operator) and ISO-014 (Soul Field).",
    "Q5_dependencies": "Depends on axioms A12.1 (moral destiny as dynamical system), A12.2 (bimodal distribution), HM.2 (binary sign variable), and the validity of the logistic equation for moral state evolution.",
    "Q6_consequences": "Forces a binary view of final destiny, predicts critical slowing near decision boundaries, implies grace as necessary for basin crossing, and generates testable predictions about population-level coherence distributions.",
    "Q7_kill": "Would be destroyed by: (1) demonstration of unimodal moral state distributions, (2) proof that logistic equation doesn't capture moral dynamics, (3) theological validation of universalism, (4) discovery of a stable third attractor, or (5) demonstration that moral orientation is continuous rather than binary."
  },
